{"name":"Broccoli Paradise","hex":"#008833","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"broccoli","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant, Brassica oleracea var. italica, of the cabbage family, Brassicaceae; especially, the tree-shaped flower and stalk that are eaten as a vegetable.","Marijuana."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of broccolo"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/broccoli"},{"term":"paradise","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The place where sanctified souls are believed to live after death.","A very pleasant place, such as a place full of lush vegetation."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“Heaven”).","Alternative letter-case form of Paradise (“the Garden of Eden”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paradise"}],"entries":[{"title":"Broccoli","blocks":[{"text":"Broccoli is an edible green plant in the cabbage family whose large flowering head, stalk and small associated leaves are eaten as a vegetable. Broccoli is classified in the Italica cultivar group of the species Brassica oleracea. Broccoli has large flower heads, or florets, usually dark green, arranged in a tree-like structure branching out from a thick stalk, which is usually light green. Leaves surround the mass of flower heads. Broccoli resembles cauliflower, a different but closely related cultivar group of the same Brassica species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Broccoli_and_cross_section_edit.jpg/330px-Broccoli_and_cross_section_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broccoli","wikidata":"Q47722"},{"title":"Paradise","blocks":[{"text":"In religion and folklore, paradise is a place of everlasting happiness, delight, and bliss. Paradisiacal notions are often laden with pastoral imagery, and may be cosmogonical, eschatological, or both, often contrasted with the miseries of human civilization: in paradise there is only peace, prosperity, and happiness. Paradise is a place of contentment, a land of luxury and fulfillment containing ever-lasting bliss and delight. Paradise is often described as a \"higher place\", the holiest place, in contrast to this world, or underworlds such as hell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg/330px-Angelico%2C_giudizio_universale_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise","wikidata":"Q3363340"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Broccoli_plants_growing_in_New_Jersey_in_April.jpg/500px-Broccoli_plants_growing_in_New_Jersey_in_April.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/34/Broccoli_plants_growing_in_New_Jersey_in_April.jpg/500px-Broccoli_plants_growing_in_New_Jersey_in_April.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Broccoli plants in a nursery","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABroccoli_plants_growing_in_New_Jersey_in_April.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Sa_broccoli_florets.jpg/500px-Sa_broccoli_florets.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Sa_broccoli_florets.jpg/1280px-Sa_broccoli_florets.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Sa_broccoli_florets.jpg/500px-Sa_broccoli_florets.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close-up of broccoli florets (click to enlarge)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASa_broccoli_florets.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia